#>wonder_shell_model_vocabulary_pocket
?["Is pocket a word to keep, now that it names the phone frame across the shell rather than one section-context component?"]{
  #:depth #!semantic ~#lens(vocabulary)
  ~#with: @plan @dom_contracts @plan_floating_chrome_stack
  ~#taste: "Fewer, truer words. A word that spread on its own may have earned its place, or may be the sprawl the glossary exists to tighten."
  ~#hypothesis: "Both July glossary candidates retire pocket into rail. Since then pocket has spread from 1 CSS and 2 JS files to 15 and 7 (bundles and generated files excluded), and CLAUDE.md names pocket chrome as a touch-target posture. That makes it a strata word for a viewport, not a rail."
  ~#tension: "Keeping pocket gives the shell vocabulary a viewport word next to shell, tier, overlay and rail. Retiring it touches about 24 CSS and JS files, some 52 .spw files, and the agent instructions."
  ~#expect: "If the hypothesis holds, the count only grows: at least 15 CSS and 7 JS files, as on 2026-10-10, with no file that uses pocket for a rail."
  ~#unblocks: "the first line of the glossary review, and whether a third candidate needs a viewport word"
  ~#probe(run)
  !probe{ "rg -l pocket public/css public/js --glob '!**/bundles/**' --glob '!**/generated/**'" }
  $%[pocket.keep_as_viewport, pocket.retire_into_rail]
  ~<"./vocabulary-census.md">
}

#>wonder_shell_model_vocabulary_glossary
?["Which glossary should the shell speak: Stagecraft, which retires chrome, Tightened Chrome, which keeps it, or either one written as Spw lines?"]{
  #:depth #!semantic ~#lens(choice)
  ~#with: @plan @census @plan_floating_chrome_stack
  ~#taste: "A glossary a reader can learn once and see used everywhere; vocabulary is codebase feeling."
  ~#tension: "A pays a large rename (chrome in 30 CSS and 28 JS files in July, and the shell/chrome/ folder) for one theatrical voice. B costs little and keeps two registers. Written as Spw lines, either one teaches the brace grammar, but a line is one more thing to keep true."
  ~#expect: "The floating pieces already took operators on 2026-11-05 ($satchel, .cauldron{}, #> for the rail), so the review will find part of the glossary already written as Spw, on the floating tier only."
  ~#unblocks: "Phase 3, one word at a time, starting in the shell/chrome/ chapters and dom-contracts.js"
  ~#probe(read)
  !probe{ "read vocabulary-census.md's candidates A and B beside floating-chrome-stack/index.spw#wonder_floating_chrome_stack_piece_operators, and write the four strata words as one Spw line each under each candidate" }
  $%[glossary.stagecraft, glossary.tightened_chrome, glossary.in_spw]
}

#>wonder_shell_model_vocabulary_attribute_grammar
?["How may one data-spw-* value fold several axes: as axis:value bundles, as role enums, both, or neither?"]{
  #:depth #!structural ~#lens(choice)
  ~#with: @plan @census
  ~#taste: "Richer values in fewer names, read the same way in DevTools and in view-source."
  ~#tension: "G1 and G2 cut the top ten families from about 195 names to about 25, but an axis:value token is a small language inside an attribute, and CLAUDE.md already forbids new data-spw-* families."
  ~#expect: "The JS-written families the census named first (accent, state-inspector, image) still carry their multi-name shape, so a migration would start where writer and reader change in one patch."
  ~#unblocks: "any attribute migration; nothing is renamed before it"
  ~#probe(read)
  !probe{ "count the data-spw-accent-* and data-spw-state-inspector-* names in public/js and public/css today, and read G1 and G2 in vocabulary-census.md against them" }
  $%[grammar.axis_bundles, grammar.role_enums, grammar.both, grammar.neither]
}
